{
 "case": "Three Mile Island (1979)",
 "oneliner": "A relief valve stuck open, but the panel light showed it closed — so the operators turned the emergency coolant DOWN. The core uncovered.",
 "summary": "Unit 2 lost coolant through a pressure-operated relief valve (PORV) that stuck open. The operators' only indication was a light wired to the valve's CLOSE command, not its actual position — so their mental model said 'closed'. Rising indications looked like 'too much water' (going solid), the thing they were trained to avoid, so they throttled High-Pressure Injection (emergency coolant). The core uncovered. No operator was incompetent and the valve-stick alone was survivable; the accident was the interaction of a stuck valve, a lying indicator, and correct-by-training operator action.",
 "source": "Kemeny Commission (1979); Perrow, Normal Accidents (1984) ch.1",
